# Commits, sign-off, and ADRs

Full detail: `CONTRIBUTING.md` and `docs/architecture/adr/README.md`.

## Sign-off is required on every commit

```bash
git commit -s
```

That adds `Signed-off-by: Name <email>`, the Developer Certificate of Origin
assertion. A commit without it does not merge.

**There is no CLA and there will not be one.** A CLA broadly licenses your
copyright to the project owner, which is what lets an owner relicense or close a
project later — several well-funded projects in this exact category did that. A
DCO asserts only that you have the right to submit the code under the existing
license, and leaves copyright with you. Relicensing Adze later would require
asking every contributor, which is the point. Do not propose adding a CLA as a
convenience.

## Conventional Commits

```
<type>(<scope>): <summary in the imperative, lowercase, no trailing period>

<body: why, not what. The diff already says what.>

Refs ADR-00NN.

Signed-off-by: Name <email>
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `perf`, `test`, `refactor`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`,
`revert`.

Scopes are package directory names plus a few cross-cutting ones:
`protocol`, `core`, `providers`, `apply`, `retrieval`, `sandbox`, `plugin-sdk`,
`mcp`, `cli`, `sdk`, `vscode`, `ide`, `hub`, `bench`, `docs`, `adr`, `ci`, `deps`.

Write the body to explain **why the change is correct**, and record bugs found
along the way — a bug discovered while writing tests is worth a paragraph,
because that paragraph is the only place the reasoning survives. Prefer several
narrowly scoped commits over one large one.

### On Windows, write long messages to a file

PowerShell mis-parses commit messages containing backticks or `${`. Write the
message to a temp file and use `git commit -s -F <file>`.

## Changesets

Anything user-visible needs one:

```bash
pnpm changeset
```

That includes protocol changes, CLI flags, tool behavior, and public types.
Internal refactors and test-only changes do not.

## When an ADR is required

Write one when the change:

- alters a boundary between packages, or what a package is allowed to import;
- changes the protocol in a way surfaces must adapt to;
- adds an outbound network call, a new dependency with security surface, or a new
  install script;
- changes the permission model, the sandbox contract, or the applier's matching
  or validation behavior;
- changes what we publish as a benchmark claim;
- **reverses an earlier decision.** A reversal gets its own ADR that supersedes
  the old one; the old one is marked `Superseded by NNNN` and kept. Deleting the
  record of a decision we changed our mind about is how a project loses the
  ability to explain itself.

An ADR states context with evidence, the decision, the alternatives that were
rejected and why, consequences split into good/bad/costs-we-accept, and the
condition that would justify revisiting it. Numbered sequentially in
`docs/architecture/adr/`.

Small changes that follow an existing ADR do not need a new one. Reference it:
`Refs ADR-0005.`

## Do not

- Amend or force-push a branch under review; push a follow-up commit instead.
- Bundle a refactor into a behavior change.
- Claim a capability works in a commit body when it is scaffolded. Point at
  `docs/roadmap.md` and name the milestone.
